# Security and Data Protection

- Never hardcode secrets or expose them in logs, diffs, documentation or examples.
- Enforce authorization server-side on every protected operation; UI hiding is not authorization.
- Check object-level access and tenant isolation, including ID substitution and cross-user access.
- Validate and normalize untrusted input at boundaries; use parameterized queries and safe output encoding.
- Avoid logging credentials, tokens, payment data or unnecessary personal information.
- Apply least privilege, secure defaults and appropriate rate limits.
- Treat file uploads, webhooks, redirects, CORS/CSRF and external callbacks as trust boundaries.
- For schema changes, inspect migration conventions, data volume, locks, backfills and rollback/forward-fix strategy.
- Do not run destructive data operations without explicit approval and a verified backup/restore plan.
